A||||17||Zoom88||Idle is hunting, seems common on 88's||||5||
Z||000000||Zoom88||11-17-2001||08:25 PM||||Ever since I have owned my 88 Formula while idling it is always surging or hunting up and down a couple hundred RPM's. Talked to the previous owner who bought it new he said it has always done it. Said he took it back to the dealer when he first got it. They told him there was a problem with all the ECM's on all the 88's and they where to come out with a fix, which they never did. Is there any truth to this ? <BR> He also said he removed the plug in the idle screw to raise the RPM's said the car would just die on him at times. Anyone have any tips where I can start troubleshooting this problem. It has a new EGR tube and O2 sensor. I am seriously wondering about the Injectors does anyone know where I can have them reconditioned ?||||reg||5
Z||000001||PBJ||11-17-2001||08:40 PM||||Sounds like you are talking about a v6, either way I would first remove the IAC and clean the pintle and bore (be sure pintle moves in and out slightly by hand) and clean throtle plate bore and butterfly. Use chemical (carb and choke cleaner) and tooth brush.<P>This is where I would start.<P>Pete.||||reg||1
Z||000002||mrfiero||11-17-2001||08:43 PM||||Hmmmm.....I have owned numerous '88 Fieros (I currently have two) and have never noticed a hunting idle, especially one that is supposedly a common trait.<P>One of my '88 GT's is an auto. with 16,500 miles and idles at a steady 900 RPM in park.  My other '88 GT is a daily driver with 117K miles and idles at 800 RPM.  (Both of these numbers are when they are fully warmed up).<P>A couple of things to look at/check are:<P>-timing<BR>-TPS<BR>-IAC<BR>-vacuum leaks (not likely)<P>Another possibility is carbon build up.  Remove the intake tube ("S" shaped rubber thing) from the throttle body and open the butterfly.  See how dirty it is on the other side.....if it is nasty then clean it up.  You will likely need to decarbonize your engine.  BG products makes some very good solvents that will remove the carbon deposits from the intake syatem.  Try your local NAPA or call Ed Parks at the Fiero Factory in Alabama......he sells these "kits".  His phone # is (256) 420-5387.  I better double check this number.......I think it is correct though.<P>Good luck.       ||||reg||1
