Z||000007||artherd||11-18-2001||03:57 AM||||Both my 88 V6s hunt(ed) but only about +-50~100rpm or so.  They also 'surge' when in gear withought the gas being applied. (ie, creeping along in traffic that I just pull off the road and eat dinner for  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Never did figure out what causes it, damn 2.8!<P>Best!<BR>Ben<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000008||grinthock||11-18-2001||08:52 AM||||I am currently working on the same kinda issue with my 87.<P>a couple things to try that are easy<P>(some as mentioned)<P>Check your IAC, clean it.<BR>MAP Sensor could be "flakey" as mine was, but it wasn't throwing a code, you could replace it (expensive) or just clean it like I did, spray some canned air into it wiggle all around shake it, clean that little vac tube, and the little metal thing going into the plenum, because it needs a good signal.<P>Hunting Idle?  Could be TPS, but I doubt it.<P>Could also be a yucky O2 sensor, if it's carboned up it will hunt more, had that on my 85.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Pyric||11-19-2001||10:53 AM||||I just finished rebuilding mine and it does the same thing, however, I have not callibrated the IAC yet because you have to drive it to do so, (and the car is not currently registered).  If calibrating it fixes the problem, I'll let you all know.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Simple Fiero||11-19-2001||01:22 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by artherd:<BR><B>Both my 88 V6s hunt(ed) but only about +-50~100rpm or so.  They also 'surge' when in gear withought the gas being applied. (ie, creeping along in traffic that I just pull off the road and eat dinner for   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Never did figure out what causes it, damn 2.8!<P>Best!<BR>Ben<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I have that surging problem in gear also , but mine is the 4 cyl. I don't think it is hurting the car but I did think about turning the idle up a quarter notch to see if that works.<BR>Craig||||reg||1
Z||000011||fldevel||11-19-2001||05:09 PM||||speeking of intakes .. does anyone know how to get the wd-40 straw outa the plenum ?? ..dont ask.. ..lol||||reg||8
Z||000012||Fierobruiser||11-19-2001||11:50 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by artherd:<BR><B>Both my 88 V6s hunt(ed) but only about +-50~100rpm or so.  They also 'surge' when in gear withought the gas being applied. (ie, creeping along in traffic that I just pull off the road and eat dinner for   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Never did figure out what causes it, damn 2.8!<P>Best!<BR>Ben<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Clutch in, seems to be the only cure for that low speed parking lot surge on my low mile 4 sp 85 GT. This is probably why I take it on trips and enjoy driving my 88 Duke every day. It averages 30 mpg in local driving while almost never using 5th gear.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Notchbacks Rule<BR>85GT(show car)<BR>88Coupe (daily driver)<BR>84 Coupe(project car)||||reg||1
Z||000013||Zoom88||11-20-2001||08:09 AM||||The surging seems to be getting worse, it does it whether it is a cold motor or hot. Timing is good. Seems to have started this a day or 2 after I replaced the O2 sensor. I may have got a bad one, got it at AutoZone. Not always the best choice !  It smells like it is burning a little rich also, runs fine while driving except there is a noticable loss of power midrange.<BR>    Thanks for all the replies, seems allot of people are experiencing this problem. It has to be something simple we are over looking. ||||reg||1
Z||000014||87GTZ34||11-20-2001||08:26 AM||||Surging is most likely caused by a change in readings from devices used as input to calibrate fuel.  Some common ones to check are TPS, MAP and the ECM temp sensor.  Any quick change in these and the ECM will respond.  To see for yourself, with the car running in neutral or park and ebrake on, slightly move the TPS actuator arm and watch what the ECM does.  These pieces can be defective due to wear, or the connections to them (including source) could be faulty.  See Ogre's Cave on checking the underhood grounds too.  GL<BR>BTW My '87 surges every once in a while as well.  I haven't tried to isolate yet as it is very infrequent.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Return of Fiero||11-20-2001||09:42 AM||||ok, i might be WAY off base for the rest of you, but my 88 2.5 stock motor did a little surging, ands it was a rotting vaccume line. it took 3 days, and 1/2 a can of starting fluid to find the problem hose, but when i replaced it, the surge went away. i never had a problem with it, till my sister toasted the clutch on me when she attempted to race me while i drove my other 88 4cyl. (not stock) and i dont think her holding the car on the hill at 3,500 rpms waiting for the light that day helped either. ah well, my surge was a vaccume line. <BR>later<BR>Myke||||reg||1
Z||000016||Pyric||11-20-2001||09:56 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Zoom88:<BR><B>The surging seems to be getting worse, it does it whether it is a cold motor or hot. Timing is good. Seems to have started this a day or 2 after I replaced the O2 sensor. I may have got a bad one, got it at AutoZone. Not always the best choice !  It smells like it is burning a little rich also, runs fine while driving except there is a noticable loss of power midrange.<BR>    Thanks for all the replies, seems allot of people are experiencing this problem. It has to be something simple we are over looking. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Does it happen right after you crank the car? or after it has been running a while?<P>The O2 sensor "supposedly" doesn't take readings until the car's engine has warmed up.  <P>Try plugging the old one back in if you've got it. If not,try unplugging it and grounding the plug.  That is supposed to let the engine run without it.  You will probably get a check engine light though.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000017||Return of Fiero||11-20-2001||09:58 AM||||I will agree with the stament "doesnt take reading till the car has warmed up" as my 84 has NO o2, and it idles fine till its warmed up, then it surges. im my case, it not "suposedly", its definetly. ill back you up there.<BR>Myke<BR>||||reg||1
